## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates mastery of fixed-popcount permutations. Tests carry propagation and ability to explain a one-shot solution without loops.

## Key Concepts
- Snoob algorithm for next with same popcount
- Carry ripple and bitwise masks
- Edge-case handling for zero or maximal permutations

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t nextBitPermutation64(uint64_t x) {
    // Assumes x != 0 and not the maximal permutation for its popcount
    uint64_t smallest = x & -x;
    uint64_t ripple = x + smallest;
    uint64_t ones = x ^ ripple;
    ones = (ones >> 2) / smallest;
    return ripple | ones;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to arbitrary bit-widths?
- Compare this approach to a loop-based generator in terms of performance and readability.

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates non-branching, low-level bit-twiddling mastery and tests the ability to work with 128-bit data using traditional C structures.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount algorithm
- Handling 128-bit values via hi/lo halves
- Non-branching reduction using masks and shifts

## Code Example
```c
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t x) {
    x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) + ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL);
    x = (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
    x = (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
    x = (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) + ((x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL);
    return (unsigned int)x;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
    return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft compact, branchless bit-twiddling across a 128-bit type.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit split into hi/lo; XOR fold reduces width.
- Branchless reduction using successive shifts and XORs.
- Final parity is the low bit after folding.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation in C-like syntax for readability
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
unsigned int parity128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t t = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  t ^= t >> 32;
  t ^= t >> 16;
  t ^= t >> 8;
  t ^= t >> 4;
  t ^= t >> 2;
  t ^= t >> 1;
  return (unsigned int)(t & 1);
}
```

